name: exit status and the special variables
boss: AndOr
flavor: Exit zero and you pass. Anything else is mine.
brief: Every command returns a number. Zero means success; anything else is a
brief: failure the command chose. $? holds the last one.
brief:
brief: That number is what && and || branch on. cmd1 && cmd2 runs the second
brief: only if the first succeeded; cmd1 || cmd2 only if it failed. This is
brief: the smallest conditional the shell has, and the most used.
brief:
brief: The other specials: $$ this shell's pid, $! the last background pid,
brief: $# how many arguments, $@ all of them, $0 the name it was invoked as.
---
q: %show% the exit status of the command you just ran
a: true; echo $?
out: ^0$
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $? holds the exit status of the immediately preceding command
---
q: {run false and then show its exit status|run the false command and check what $? becomes|execute false, then print its exit code}
a: false; echo $?
out: ^1$
ref: false
teach: false
hint: false always exits 1; it has no other job
---
q: {run true and confirm its exit status is zero|run true and verify $? comes back 0|execute true, then check its exit code is zero}
a: true; echo $?
out: ^0$
ref: true
teach: true
hint: true always exits 0, no matter what
---
q: %show% @word@ only if @log@ contains @sev@
a: grep -q @sev@ @log@ && echo @word@
out: @word@
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: -q silences grep's output, leaving only its exit status for &&
---
q: %show% "missing" only if @log@ does NOT contain the word nonexistent
a: grep -q nonexistent @log@ || echo missing
out: ^missing$
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: || runs its right side only when the left side fails
---
q: %show% found if @sev@ is in @log@, and missing if it is not, in one line
a: grep -q @sev@ @log@ && echo found || echo missing
out: ^found$
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: chain && and || together to cover both outcomes in one line
---
q: %show% this shell's own process id
a: echo $$
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $$ is this shell's own pid, fixed for its whole lifetime
---
q: {start sleep 1 in the background and print the pid it was given|background sleep 1, then print its pid|run sleep 1 as a background job and show what pid it got}
a: sleep 1 & echo $!
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
hint: an ampersand backgrounds; $! holds the pid it was just given
---
q: {exit the shell with status 0|end the shell with exit status 0|leave the shell, setting its exit status to 0}
a: exit 0
ref: exit
teach: exit
hint: exit takes the status as its argument; with none it reuses $?
---
q: {exit the shell with status 3|end the shell with exit status 3|leave the shell, setting its exit status to 3}
a: exit 3
ref: exit
teach: exit
hint: any number 0-255; zero means success and everything else is yours to define
---
q: {invert an exit status -- succeed when grep finds nothing in @log@|flip a status so grep finding nothing counts as success in @log@|make "no match in @log@" read as success rather than failure}
a: ! grep -q nonexistent @log@
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: a leading ! flips the status
---
q: %make% the shell stop at the first command that fails
a: set -e
ref: set
teach: set -e
hint: -e makes any failing command end the script immediately
---
q: %make% the shell treat an unset variable as an error rather than an empty string
a: set -u
ref: set
teach: set -u
hint: -u turns a reference to an unset variable into a hard error
---
q: %make% the shell print each command before running it
a: set -x
ref: set
teach: set -x
hint: this is the shell debugger, and it is usually enough
---
q: %turn% command tracing back off
a: set +x
ref: set
teach: set
hint: + turns an option off, the mirror of - turning it on
---
q: %show% the shell's current option settings
a: set -o
ref: set
teach: set -o
hint: -o with no name lists every option and whether it's on or off
